-What in the world is it????
----------------------------

    Basically this is a dir command with an interface to Virus Checker and
a BBS style output as options.  I didn't really mean for it to be a  dir
command but I needed a recursion subroutine and the only good one I  could
find was in Wouter van Oortmerssen's 'D' program.  I rewrote alot of that
program to accomodate the virus checking and the AREXX ports but  the core
remains and is a pretty good dir command.

      

Here are the DOCS for Wouter's D utility, I have added the new commands for
EVC...

+---------------------------------------+
|	D.E				|
+---------------------------------------+
needs v37

Universal directory lister suplied with Amiga E. called with no
arguments, by just typing "d", it lists the current directory.
template:

DIR,REC/S,COL/K/N,VCHECK/S,BBS/S,FILE/K,MASTER/S,SIZE/S,NOSORT/S,NOFILES/S,
NODIRS/S,FULL/S,NOANSI/S,TARGET/K,DO/K/F

DIR		specifies an optional path to the dir you wish to list.
		may contain standard wildcard patterns like #?~[]%() etc.
		I am not certian the wildcards will work in BBS mode.
REC		specifies that subdirectories should be listed recursively.
COL <num>	where n=1..3, by default, D list dirs in three columns to
		keep all nice and compact. specify 1 or 2 if you like.
*BBS		Output to a BBS, this will work in a CLI window of course
		but is ment for display on the BBS
                         
*VCHECK		Check every listed file for viruses.  This is ment for the
		BBS option but can be listed alone.  In CLI mode it will
                check each file for viruses as it lists it.  Pretty neat 
                option for regular use actually.  On my 28MHz 030 it does
                not seem to slow things down a bit.  Normally VCHECK turns
                on the virus checking.  If there is a ENV: varible called
                EVC_VCHECK containing the word YES then this is reversed,
                the switch VCHECK will turn OFF the virus checking.  This
                is so you do not have to specify VCHECK every time and
                always want files checked for viruses as I do.  It's a bit
                extra time but you should never miss a virus. 
*MASTER       	This switch tells EVC that you are using Excelsior! BBS and
		to do some logging to the MASTER console windows AREXX port.
*FILE <name>	This allows you to inform EVC of the original archive's
		name.  This won't affect the running of EVC, it will alter
		the BBS display and the logging.  This SHOULD be a FULL
		path and file name.
*TRASH <path>   If the file fails the virus check then it is moved here. 
		This only works if you have specified a file with the FILE
		comand.  
SIZE		reports the size of each dir as it is being listed. note that
		combined with REC gives sizes of whole dir (sub-)trees.
		Disabled in BBS mode
NOSORT		by default, dirs are sorted before display. disable this with
		the NOSORT switch.
NOFILES		displays just dirs
NODIRS		displays just files. Usless in VCHECK and BBS modes.
FULL		lists full path instead of just filename
NOANSI		doesn't use ansi display codes while printing.  Diabled in
                BBS mode.
TARGET <dir>	specifies a target directory for use with DO. should
		end in either "/" or ":".  Disabled in BBS & VCHECK modes.
DO <comline>	specifies a commandline for automatic script generation.
		note that this uses up the rest of D's commandline.
		Disabled in BBS and VCHECK modes.

something should be said on the script feature: it enables you
to perform repetitive tasks on whole dirs, or dir-trees. existing
utilities that enabled you to do such tasks where mostly not
flexible enough; d enables you to use the REC keyword in combination
with scripts, allows for variable extensions: use <file>.o if
the original name was <file>.s, and the spec. of a target:
resulting files from the operation are placed in another dir, which
can be a complete mirror image of another dir-tree. makedir
statements are inserted if target: is empty.

following format codes may be used in <commandline>:

%s is file (filename+path)
%f is file WITHOUT extension
%r is file without extension, but with leading <dir> replaced by
   <target> (usefull if <commandline> allows for an outputfile)
%> or %< %>> etc. prevents the shell from thinking ">" is a redirection
   for EVC, instead of <commandline>

a complex example:
you wish to have a complete ascii reference of the emodules:
directory, recursively, and with the resulting .txt files
as a mirror-image directory structure somewhere else.

1> EVC >ram:script emodules: REC TARGET=t:mods/ DO showmodule %>%r.txt %s
1> execute ram:script

will do that for you.
for any file like "emodules:exec/io.m" D will make a line like:
"showmodule  >t:mods/exec/io.txt emodules:exec/io.m"

other examples:	EVC >mydirlist dh0: COL=2 SIZE REC NOANSI
		EVC docs: DO type >prt: %s
		EVC asm: TARGET=obj: DO genam %s -o%r.o
                EVC C: VCHECK
                EVC BBS: REC VCHECK SIZE
                 
See the EVC???.scp files in teh evc/s directory for more examples of how
the virus checking and BBS output work.
-------------

To install as a BBS virus checker.... well.....

-COPY evc/s/* TO s:
       
-Change the INTEGRITY check command of the ARCHIVERS area in your
BBSCONFIG. I have included a screenshot of my BBSCONFIG for you to compare
to.  You need to change the integrity check of each of your archive types. 

-Alter your ENGLISH (or whatever) file, there should be a line with the
'Passed.' word on it.  This is the word that comes up after the 'Checking
File Integrity...' string comes up.  Make that line 'Passed.
\#1REXX:EVC.REXX\' (without the quotes of course).  This will test the
archive integrity and if it passes then it will check the file for viruses.
***The REXX:EVC.REXX is built by EVC.SCP and should NOT exist as of yet.  It
   will be created when someone uploads something.

-Make sure EVC and EVC_REQ are in your path somewhere.  I placed them in
my C: directory.

-Reboot and enjoy
----

    If the program finds a virus it SHOULD, and I say SHOULD because I have
no viruses to test it on.  I tested it by fooling it into thinking that all
files have viruses and it worked but I won't be 100% sure until someone
finds a virus, well anyway, it SHOULD open a requestor on the WorkBench
saying that it found a virus, this requestor is a sepreate task (EVC_REQ)
and will  not lock up the BBS or anything else.  It will also create a log
to your BBSs MAINT logfile.  I will improve on this in a later regisered
version.

